00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.280
Once you have the entry discipline, the next layer is making sure any single position can't hurt

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:48.340
the whole book. Let's move into position sizing. Imagine you allocated $1000 to a single energy

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:54.280
name last week when oil spiked on Middle East headlines. Your order filled at the opening print,

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.740
but the position size left no room for the 7% reversal that followed.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:05.540
The mechanism works through simple arithmetic. A 2% portfolio allocation limits any single

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:12.180
loss to roughly $20 on a $1000 account, even if the stop is hit at 10% below entry.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:20.980
A 10% allocation turns that same 10% drop into a $100 hit that compounds across correlated holdings.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.420
Professionals always calculate the maximum acceptable loss in dollar terms first.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.360
Then they back into share quantity using the distance to their chosen stop, whether fixed,

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.760
trailing, or volatility-based. The biggest mistake with position sizing is letting a promising setup

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:44.980
dictate allocation instead of the other way around. Instead, always size every new position so that

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:52.100
a full stop out equals no more than 1% of total portfolio value. Correlation math adds another layer.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.500
When energy names move together on the same geopolitical headline, even properly-sized

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:04.240
individual stops can still produce a portfolio-level drawdown that exceeds the 1% rule if 3 or 4

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:11.220
holdings trigger simultaneously. The kelly criterion simplified for retail use suggests betting no more

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:17.280
than the edge divided by the odds against. In practice, that means shrinking position size further

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:23.560
when volatility spikes above the 20-day average. Trailing stops adjust automatically as price

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:29.860
moves in your favor, yet they still require an initial hard stop to define the maximum loss before

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:36.480
the trail begins. Portfolio drawdowns become psychologically easier to manage when each position

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:43.020
is caped at 1% risk. The investor can review the setup calmly rather than reacting to a single

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:50.080
oversized loss. The concrete misconception to avoid is treating every new idea as equally important.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:56.540
Instead, always rank setups by conviction and allocate risk dollars accordingly so that only

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:02.340
the highest conviction names receive the full 1% allocation. With sizing rules in place,

